  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":1,"t":"single_choice","p":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 4 and uₙ₊₁ = uₙ + 3. What is the value of u₄?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 4 and uₙ₊₁ = uₙ + 3. What is the value of u₄?"}]}]},"e":"u₁ = 4; u₂ = 4 + 3 = 7; u₃ = 7 + 3 = 10; u₄ = 10 + 3 = 13.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":1,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Add 3 each step, starting from 4.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["10","13","16","7"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Write the terms one at a time: 4, then +3…","Each step adds the same amount — apply it to the term you just found."]},"marking":""},{"n":2,"t":"single_choice","p":"Which explicit formula describes the sequence defined by u₁ = 5 and uₙ₊₁ = uₙ + 4?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which explicit formula describes the sequence defined by u₁ = 5 and uₙ₊₁ = uₙ + 4?"}]}]},"e":"First term 5, common difference 4. uₙ = 5 + (n − 1) × 4 = 4n + 1 (check: n = 1 → 5 ✓; n = 2 → 9 ✓).\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":2,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"uₙ = first term + (n − 1) × difference.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["uₙ = n + 4","uₙ = 5n + 4","uₙ = 4n + 5","uₙ = 4n + 1"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["The sequence is 5, 9, 13, … — fit the formula to it.","Start from a₁ and the difference, expand, and simplify."]},"marking":""},{"n":3,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two traders in a market launch a price alert. Each of them passes it to 3 new people, and every one of those people passes it to 3 new people the next hour, so the number of newly informed people is 2, 6, 18, 54, … A rule for this sequence is u₁ = 2 and uₙ = 3 × uₙ₋₁ for n ≥ 2. What is u₅?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two traders in a market launch a price alert. Each of them passes it to 3 new people, and every one of those people passes it to 3 new people the next hour, so the number of newly informed people is 2, 6, 18, 54, … A rule for this sequence is u₁ = 2 and uₙ = 3 × uₙ₋₁ for n ≥ 2. What is u₅?"}]}]},"e":"u₁ = 2; u₂ = 6; u₃ = 18; u₄ = 54; u₅ = 3 × 54 = 162.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":3,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Multiply by 3, five times total.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["54","243","486","162"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Keep multiplying: 2 → 6 → 18 → 54 → …","Keep multiplying by the ratio until you reach the fifth term."]},"marking":""},{"n":4,"t":"single_choice","p":"A student writes the explicit formula for the sequence 7, 11, 15, 19, … as uₙ = 7 + 4n. Which statement best describes the error?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A student writes the explicit formula for the sequence 7, 11, 15, 19, … as uₙ = 7 + 4n. Which statement best describes the error?"}]}]},"e":"uₙ = 7 + 4n gives u₁ = 11 ≠ 7. The sequence is 7 + (n − 1) × 4 = 4n + 3 (check: n = 1 → 7 ✓, n = 2 → 11 ✓). So the formula was shifted by one term.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":4,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Test the formula at n = 1.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["The formula is shifted by one term: it gives 11 when n = 1, so it should be uₙ = 7 + 4(n − 1)","There is no error: the formula gives 7, 11, 15, 19, …","The difference is wrong; the formula should be uₙ = 7 + 3n","The 4 should be multiplied by the first term: uₙ = 7 × 4n"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Substitute n = 1 into the student’s formula.","Substitute n = 1 into both the student’s rule and a corrected shift — which reproduces the printed terms?"]},"marking":""},{"n":5,"t":"single_choice","p":"The explicit rule of a sequence is uₙ = 6n − 1. Which recursive definition produces exactly the same sequence?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The explicit rule of a sequence is uₙ = 6n − 1. Which recursive definition produces exactly the same sequence?"}]}]},"e":"u₁ = 6(1) − 1 = 5, and the constant difference is 6 (coefficients of n), so uₙ = uₙ₋₁ + 6 with u₁ = 5. Check: 5, 11, 17, … = 6n − 1 ✓.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":5,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Find u₁ and the common difference from 6n − 1.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["u₁ = 5, uₙ = 3 × uₙ₋₁","u₁ = −1, uₙ = uₙ₋₁ + 6","u₁ = 6, uₙ = uₙ₋₁ + 6","u₁ = 5, uₙ = uₙ₋₁ + 6"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Evaluate the explicit rule at n = 1 to get the first term.","The step never changes — express each term as the previous one plus it."]},"marking":""},{"n":6,"t":"single_choice","p":"The figures are made with matchsticks: figure 1 uses 4 sticks, figure 2 uses 7 sticks and figure 3 uses 10 sticks. Each new figure adds one more square to the row. Which recursive rule gives the number of sticks Sₙ in figure n?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The figures are made with matchsticks: figure 1 uses 4 sticks, figure 2 uses 7 sticks and figure 3 uses 10 sticks. Each new figure adds one more square to the row. Which recursive rule gives the number of sticks Sₙ in figure n?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp5/q06_matchsticks.jpg","alt":"Figures 1, 2 and 3 built from matchsticks (4, 7 and 10 sticks)","width":96}}]},"e":"Sticks used: 4, 7, 10 — each figure adds 3 sticks (two for the vertical sides shared, one new square needs 3 extra). Starting from 4: Sₙ = Sₙ₋₁ + 3.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":6,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Count the added sticks: 4 → 7 → 10.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["S₁ = 4, Sₙ = 3 × Sₙ₋₁","S₁ = 4, Sₙ = Sₙ₋₁ + 3","S₁ = 4, Sₙ = Sₙ₋₁ + 4","S₁ = 3, Sₙ = Sₙ₋₁ + 4"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["The difference between successive figures is 3.","Express each figure’s stick count as the previous one plus the constant increase."],"figure":"myp5_q06_matchsticks"},"marking":""},{"n":7,"t":"single_choice","p":"A mobile data subscription costs 12 000 FCFA in the first month. From the second month onwards, the price increases by 1 500 FCFA every month. Which recursive rule gives the monthly price uₙ, in FCFA?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A mobile data subscription costs 12 000 FCFA in the first month. From the second month onwards, the price increases by 1 500 FCFA every month. Which recursive rule gives the monthly price uₙ, in FCFA?"}]}]},"e":"First month 12 000; every later month adds 1 500: u₁ = 12 000, uₙ = uₙ₋₁ + 1 500. Option A is explicit, not recursive, and gives 13 500 for n = 1.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":7,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"A recursive rule names the first term and how to get the next.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["uₙ = 12 000 + 1 500n for n ≥ 1","u₁ = 12 000, uₙ = uₙ₋₁ + 1 500","u₁ = 12 000, uₙ = 1 500 × uₙ₋₁","u₁ = 1 500, uₙ = uₙ₋₁ + 12 000"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["‘Recursive’ means each term comes from the previous one.","First term from the starting deposit; the step is the monthly increase."]},"marking":""},{"n":8,"t":"single_choice","p":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 3 and uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ − 1. What is u₅?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 3 and uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ − 1. What is u₅?"}]}]},"e":"u₂ = 2×3 − 1 = 5; u₃ = 2×5 − 1 = 9; u₄ = 2×9 − 1 = 17; u₅ = 2×17 − 1 = 33.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":8,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Iterate carefully, one step at a time.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["65","31","33","17"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["u₂ = 5 and u₃ = 9.","Apply the same doubling-then-subtract-1 rule once more."]},"marking":""},{"n":9,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two rules are given for the same kind of sequence: uₙ = 5n − 2 and v₁ = 3, vₙ₊₁ = vₙ + 5. Which statement is true?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two rules are given for the same kind of sequence: uₙ = 5n − 2 and v₁ = 3, vₙ₊₁ = vₙ + 5. Which statement is true?"}]}]},"e":"u: u₁ = 3, u₂ = 8, u₃ = 13, … (start 3, step 5). v: v₁ = 3, v₂ = 8, v₃ = 13, … — identical: same first term, same step, so uₙ = vₙ for all n ≥ 1.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Recursive and Explicit Formulas","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":9,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"List the first three terms of each.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["Every term of v is 2 more than the matching term of u","The two sequences have the same first term but grow at different rates","The two rules generate the same sequence: uₙ = vₙ for every n ≥ 1","uₙ and vₙ are equal only when n is even"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["u₁ = 3 and v₁ = 3 — same start.","Compare what each rule does at every step — same start, same operation?"]},"marking":""},{"n":10,"t":"single_choice","p":"A weather balloon begins to descend at a constant rate. The first reading of its height is 800 m and every reading after that is 150 m lower. The readings form a linear sequence. What is the height at the 6th reading?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A weather balloon begins to descend at a constant rate. The first reading of its height is 800 m and every reading after that is 150 m lower. The readings form a linear sequence. What is the height at the 6th reading?"}]}]},"e":"Reading n: height = 800 − (n − 1) × 150. For n = 6: 800 − 5 × 150 = 800 − 750 = 50 m.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Linear Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":10,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"800 − 5 lots of 150.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["50 m","200 m","−100 m","350 m"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["From the first to the 6th reading there are 5 steps.","Subtract five equal steps of 150 from the opening height."]},"marking":""},{"n":11,"t":"single_choice","p":"The table gives the first four terms of a linear sequence. Which rule gives its nth term?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The table gives the first four terms of a linear sequence. Which rule gives its nth term?"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"n"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4"}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"uₙ"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7"}]},{"type":"tableCell","content":[{"type":"text","text":"9"}]}]}]}]},"e":"Terms 3, 5, 7, 9: common difference 2, so uₙ = 3 + (n − 1) × 2 = 2n + 1 (check n = 1 → 3 ✓).\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Linear Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":11,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Find the difference, then fit uₙ = a + (n − 1)d.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["uₙ = 2n + 1","uₙ = 3n","uₙ = n + 2","uₙ = 2n − 1"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["The terms increase by 2 each step.","uₙ = 3 + (n − 1)·2 = 2n + 1."]},"marking":""},{"n":12,"t":"single_choice","p":"The terms of a linear sequence are plotted as points (n, uₙ) and all the points lie on the line u = 4n − 1. What is the first term of the sequence?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The terms of a linear sequence are plotted as points (n, uₙ) and all the points lie on the line u = 4n − 1. What is the first term of the sequence?"}]}]},"e":"The first term is u₁ = 4 × 1 − 1 = 3.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Linear Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":12,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"The first term is u at n = 1.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["4","3","7","−1"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Read the line rule at n = 1.","Substitute n = 1 into the rule."]},"marking":""},{"n":13,"t":"single_choice","p":"A taxi charges a fixed pickup fee of 1 000 FCFA and then 500 FCFA for every kilometre travelled. The fares for trips of 1 km, 2 km, 3 km, … form a linear sequence. What is the fare for a trip of 9 km?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A taxi charges a fixed pickup fee of 1 000 FCFA and then 500 FCFA for every kilometre travelled. The fares for trips of 1 km, 2 km, 3 km, … form a linear sequence. What is the fare for a trip of 9 km?"}]}]},"e":"Fare for n km = 1000 + 500n. For 9 km: 1000 + 500 × 9 = 1000 + 4500 = 5500 FCFA.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Linear Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":13,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"1000 + 500 per kilometre.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["5 000 FCFA","5 500 FCFA","6 000 FCFA","4 500 FCFA"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Fixed part 1000, then 500 for each of the 9 km.","Fixed charge plus the per-km rate times the distance."]},"marking":""},{"n":14,"t":"single_choice","p":"A linear sequence has u₄ = 19 and u₉ = 44. Which pair gives the first term and the common difference?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A linear sequence has u₄ = 19 and u₉ = 44. Which pair gives the first term and the common difference?"}]}]},"e":"From u₄ to u₉ there are 5 steps: (44 − 19) ÷ 5 = 5 = d. Then u₁ = 19 − 3 × 5 = 4. Check: 4, 9, 14, 19 ✓, u₉ = 4 + 8 × 5 = 44 ✓.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Linear Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":14,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Use the two known terms to find d first.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["u₁ = 4, d = 25","u₁ = 19, d = 5","u₁ = 4, d = 5","u₁ = 14, d = 5"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Five steps from u₄ to u₉ cover 25 marks.","Five steps from u₄ to u₉ cover the change — divide, then back-substitute."]},"marking":""},{"n":15,"t":"single_choice","p":"A water tank is drained at a constant rate and its volume is recorded every 10 minutes. The first reading is 3 200 L and each following reading is 150 L lower. Which is the first reading below 1 500 L?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A water tank is drained at a constant rate and its volume is recorded every 10 minutes. The first reading is 3 200 L and each following reading is 150 L lower. Which is the first reading below 1 500 L?"}]}]},"e":"The kth reading is 3200 − (k − 1) × 150. Reading 12: 3200 − 1650 = 1550 (not below 1500). Reading 13: 3200 − 1800 = 1400 < 1500 ✓.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Linear Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":15,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Compute the readings until one drops below 1500.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["the 12th reading","the 11th reading","the 14th reading","the 13th reading"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Write out the readings from the formula until one drops below 1500.","The first reading below 1500 is the position you want."]},"marking":""},{"n":16,"t":"single_choice","p":"Two linear sequences are uₙ = 4n + 3 and vₙ = 4n − 5. Which statement is correct?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two linear sequences are uₙ = 4n + 3 and vₙ = 4n − 5. Which statement is correct?"}]}]},"e":"Both have common difference 4; vₙ = uₙ − 8 always, so the terms differ by exactly 8 at every position and no term coincides. Rates of increase are identical, and u₂ = 11 ≠ v₂ = 3.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Linear Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":16,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Compare the step and the gap between uₙ and vₙ.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["u₂ = v₂, so the two sequences are equal from the second term onwards","Both increase by 4 each step and their terms always differ by 8, so no term of one sequence equals a term of the other","Sequence v increases faster than sequence u","The sequences have equal terms when n = 8"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Both sequences add 4 per step.","Subtract the two rules — what remains is a constant. What is it?"]},"marking":""},{"n":17,"t":"single_choice","p":"A freezer stops working and its temperature rises at a constant rate. The readings, in °C, are −15, −9, −3, 3, … What is the common difference of this arithmetic sequence?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A freezer stops working and its temperature rises at a constant rate. The readings, in °C, are −15, −9, −3, 3, … What is the common difference of this arithmetic sequence?"}]}]},"e":"−9 − (−15) = 6; −3 − (−9) = 6; 3 − (−3) = 6. The common difference is 6 °C.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":17,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Subtract consecutive terms.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["12 °C","−6 °C","18 °C","6 °C"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Compare the first two readings.","The readings climb by the same amount at every step."]},"marking":""},{"n":18,"t":"single_choice","p":"On the first day of the season a fruit seller sells 7 baskets of mangoes. Every day after that he sells 4 baskets more than the day before. How many baskets does he sell on the 12th day?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On the first day of the season a fruit seller sells 7 baskets of mangoes. Every day after that he sells 4 baskets more than the day before. How many baskets does he sell on the 12th day?"}]}]},"e":"Day 12 = 7 + (12 − 1) × 4 = 7 + 44 = 51 baskets.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":18,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"11 increases of 4 from day 1.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["43","55","51","48"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["From day 1 to day 12 there are 11 steps.","First reading plus eleven steps of the daily increase."]},"marking":""},{"n":19,"t":"single_choice","p":"The nth term of an arithmetic sequence is aₙ = 19 − 3n. What are the first term and the common difference?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The nth term of an arithmetic sequence is aₙ = 19 − 3n. What are the first term and the common difference?"}]}]},"e":"a₁ = 19 − 3×1 = 16. Between consecutive terms, aₙ₊₁ − aₙ = −3, so d = −3. Check: 16, 13, 10, …\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Evaluate at n = 1 and look at the coefficient of n.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["a₁ = 16, d = 3","a₁ = 19, d = −3","a₁ = 16, d = −3","a₁ = 13, d = −3"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Compare the rule with a₁ + (n − 1)d to read a₁.","The coefficient attached to n is the common difference."]},"marking":""},{"n":20,"t":"single_choice","p":"Which term of the arithmetic sequence 3, 7, 11, … is 127?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which term of the arithmetic sequence 3, 7, 11, … is 127?"}]}]},"e":"3 + (n − 1) × 4 = 127 → (n − 1) × 4 = 124 → n − 1 = 31 → n = 32.\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":20,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Solve 3 + (n − 1)·4 = 127.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["the 32nd","the 124th","the 31st","the 33rd"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["Subtract the first term: 127 − 3 = 124.","Divide the change by d — that many steps after term 1."]},"marking":""},{"n":21,"t":"single_choice","p":"In a stand at a stadium, the first row has 38 seats. Every row after that has 5 more seats than the row before it. How many seats are in the 20th row?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a stand at a stadium, the first row has 38 seats. Every row after that has 5 more seats than the row before it. How many seats are in the 20th row?"}]}]},"e":"Row 20 = 38 + (20 − 1) × 5 = 38 + 95 = 133 seats.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":21,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"19 steps × 5 extra seats.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["128","138","133","100"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["From row 1 to row 20: 19 additions of 5.","First row plus nineteen steps of the daily addition."]},"marking":""},{"n":22,"t":"single_choice","p":"The value of a laptop decreases by the same amount every year. After 1 year it is worth 390 000 FCFA and after 3 years it is worth 330 000 FCFA. What is the yearly decrease, and what was the original price?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The value of a laptop decreases by the same amount every year. After 1 year it is worth 390 000 FCFA and after 3 years it is worth 330 000 FCFA. What is the yearly decrease, and what was the original price?"}]}]},"e":"Between year 1 and year 3 (2 years) the drop is 60 000, so the yearly decrease is 30 000. Original price = 390 000 + 30 000 = 420 000 FCFA.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":22,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"Halve the two-year drop, then add one year back.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["20 000 FCFA and 450 000 FCFA","60 000 FCFA and 390 000 FCFA","30 000 FCFA and 390 000 FCFA","30 000 FCFA and 420 000 FCFA"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["Divide the change between the two known prices by the number of steps.","From the earlier price, walk one step back to the year before it."]},"marking":""},{"n":23,"t":"single_choice","p":"A student claims that the sequence with nth term aₙ = n² − n + 4 is arithmetic. Which is the correct response?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A student claims that the sequence with nth term aₙ = n² − n + 4 is arithmetic. Which is the correct response?"}]}]},"e":"Terms: 4, 6, 10, 16, … Differences: 2, 4, 6 — not constant. The sequence is quadratic, not arithmetic. The first term being 4 says nothing about arithmeticity.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"medium","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":23,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Compute the terms and their differences.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["It is arithmetic with common difference 2","It is not arithmetic because the first term is 4","It is not arithmetic: the differences are 2, 4, 6, …, which are not constant","It is arithmetic with common difference 1"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["a₁ = 4, a₂ = 6, a₃ = 10, a₄ = 16.","Are the gaps between consecutive terms the same each time?"]},"marking":""},{"n":24,"t":"single_choice","p":"The three expressions k + 2, 3k − 1 and 7k + 1, in this order, are three consecutive terms of an arithmetic sequence. What is the value of k?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The three expressions k + 2, 3k − 1 and 7k + 1, in this order, are three consecutive terms of an arithmetic sequence. What is the value of k?"}]}]},"e":"In an arithmetic sequence the middle term is the average of its neighbours: 2(3k − 1) = (k + 2) + (7k + 1) → 6k − 2 = 8k + 3 → −5 = 2k → k = −2.5.\\n\\nAnswer: D","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":24,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","next_time_hint":"Use the equal-difference condition.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["2.5","−0.5","−5","−2.5"],"option_key":[4],"hints":["(3k − 1) − (k + 2) must equal (7k + 1) − (3k − 1).","Simplify to one equation in k and solve it."]},"marking":""},{"n":25,"t":"single_choice","p":"The price of a plot of land increases by the same amount every year. It was 3 500 000 FCFA in 2018 and 4 700 000 FCFA in 2022. In which year will the price first exceed 6 000 000 FCFA?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The price of a plot of land increases by the same amount every year. It was 3 500 000 FCFA in 2018 and 4 700 000 FCFA in 2022. In which year will the price first exceed 6 000 000 FCFA?"}]}]},"e":"Increase over 4 years (2018→2022): 1 200 000 → 300 000 per year. From 2022: after n more years the price is 4 700 000 + 300 000n. Need > 6 000 000 → n > 4.33 → n = 5 → 2027 (2027 price = 6 200 000; 2026 = 5 900 000, not yet).\\n\\nAnswer: A","m":1,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":25,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","next_time_hint":"300 000 per year; count years from 2022.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["2027","2026","2029","2028"],"option_key":[1],"hints":["(4 700 000 − 3 500 000) ÷ 4 = 300 000 FCFA per year.","From 2022, keep adding the yearly rise until the total passes 6 000 000."]},"marking":""},{"n":26,"t":"single_choice","p":"A gardener lays square flower beds made of 1 m² tiles: the first bed uses 1 tile, the second 4 tiles, the third 9 tiles and the fourth 16 tiles. How many tiles are in the 5th square bed?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A gardener lays square flower beds made of 1 m² tiles: the first bed uses 1 tile, the second 4 tiles, the third 9 tiles and the fourth 16 tiles. How many tiles are in the 5th square bed?"}]},{"type":"image","attrs":{"src":"/exam-figures/myp5/q26_beds.jpg","alt":"Square grids of 1, 4, 9 and 16 tiles","width":96}}]},"e":"The pattern is 1², 2², 3², 4², … — bed n uses n² tiles. The 5th bed uses 25 tiles.\\n\\nAnswer: B","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":26,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","next_time_hint":"Square numbers: 1, 4, 9, 16, …","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["20","25","36","24"],"option_key":[2],"hints":["Each bed is an n × n square of tiles.","Figure 5 is a 5 by 5 square of tiles."],"figure":"myp5_q26_beds"},"marking":""},{"n":27,"t":"single_choice","p":"How can you tell, from a list of terms, that a sequence is quadratic rather than linear?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How can you tell, from a list of terms, that a sequence is quadratic rather than linear?"}]}]},"e":"Linear ⇒ constant first differences. Quadratic ⇒ constant, non-zero second differences. A constant ratio signals a geometric sequence.\\n\\nAnswer: C","m":1,"df":"easy","tp":"Quadratic Sequences","cfg":{"source":"myp5-full-formal-assessment-2026","question_number":27,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","next_time_hint":"Think about first and second differences.","assessment_style":"formal_assessment","option_labels":["The ratio between consecutive terms is constant","Its first differences are constant","Its second differences are constant and not equal to zero","Its terms keep getting larger and larger"],"option_key":[3],"hints":["Linear sequences have constant first differences.","Quadratic sequences have constant non-zero second differences."]},"marking":""}]'::jsonb) as x
